HB.add({
  id: "03",
  intro: `The offline pipeline starts here. Ingestion is how documents get <b>into</b> the system and how they <b>stay correct</b> over time: where data comes from (files, websites, databases, SaaS tools), how changes, duplicates and deletes are handled, and the governance rules — metadata, permissions, privacy, ownership — that must be attached before anything is indexed. Mistakes made here (missing permissions, stale copies, duplicates) cannot be fixed later by a better model.`,
  groups: [
    { id: "3.1", title: "Sources & Connectors", items: [
      { t: `File Loaders (PDF, DOCX, PPTX, HTML)`,
        s: `Code that opens a file of a given format and returns its text, structure and basic metadata.`,
        h: `Each format needs its own reader. PDFs are read with PyMuPDF, pdfplumber or Docling (text layer, positions, layout). DOCX files are zipped XML, so python-docx can read paragraphs, headings and tables directly. PPTX files are read slide by slide with python-pptx (titles, text boxes, notes). HTML is parsed with BeautifulSoup or trafilatura. A good loader returns text plus structure (headings, tables, page or slide numbers) and metadata, and it detects the real file type from the bytes (magic numbers), never from the file name alone.`,
        u: `Use file loaders in upload-based systems (users drag documents into a portal), in connectors that download files from Drive or SharePoint, and in batch imports of document archives. Choose the loader per format and per difficulty: a fast simple reader for clean files, a layout-aware parser for complex PDFs, and OCR for scans. Keep loaders behind one interface so new formats can be added without touching the rest of the pipeline, and log which loader handled each file.`,
        p: `A single generic "extract text" function loses tables, slide order, headings and page numbers, and often crashes on unexpected formats or renamed files. The result is chunks without structure, citations without pages, and silent gaps where files failed to load. Format-specific loaders keep the structure that chunking and citations need, fail loudly on unsupported files, and protect against files pretending to be another type, such as an executable renamed to ".pdf". Loader errors also need clear reporting: a user who uploads a scanned PDF should be told that OCR ran (or failed), not left wondering why the document never answers questions.`,
        e: `An HR portal accepts PDF policies, DOCX forms and PPTX training decks. Each upload is type-checked with python-magic and sent to the matching loader: Docling for the PDF (with its tables), python-docx for the form (with headings), python-pptx for the deck (slide titles and speaker notes). A file named "policy.pdf" that is really a ZIP archive is rejected with a clear error instead of producing garbage chunks. The loader also records the page count and the parser used, so a later complaint about a missing table can be traced to the exact parsing step.`,
        c: `
import magic, fitz, docx           # python-magic, PyMuPDF, python-docx
def load(path):
    kind = magic.from_file(path, mime=True)
    if kind == "application/pdf":
        return [(p.number + 1, p.get_text()) for p in fitz.open(path)]
    if kind.endswith("wordprocessingml.document"):
        return [(None, "\\n".join(par.text for par in docx.Document(path).paragraphs))]
    raise ValueError(f"unsupported type {kind}")` },
      { t: `Web Crawling / Scraping`,
        s: `Automatically visiting web pages, following links, and extracting their main text for indexing.`,
        h: `A crawler starts from seed URLs or a sitemap, respects robots.txt and rate limits, downloads each page, extracts the main content (dropping menus, footers and ads with tools like trafilatura), and queues new links that stay within the allowed domains and paths. It records the URL, title, last-modified date and ETag for each page. On later runs it sends conditional requests (If-None-Match / If-Modified-Since) so unchanged pages are skipped, and it removes pages that now return 404.`,
        u: `Use crawling for public documentation sites, help centres, product pages, internal wikis without a usable API, and partner portals you are allowed to index. Prefer a site's API or export when one exists, because crawled HTML is noisier. Schedule crawls by how often the content changes (daily for help articles, weekly for stable docs) and limit depth and domains so the crawler does not wander into unrelated sites. Store the canonical URL of every page so citations link back to the live source. Exclude login pages, search-result pages and archives that duplicate content.`,
        p: `Knowledge is often spread over hundreds or thousands of web pages that nobody will upload by hand, and it changes constantly. Without crawling, the RAG index misses new articles and keeps answering from deleted ones. A well-behaved incremental crawler keeps the index current with little effort, while boilerplate removal makes sure every chunk contains real content rather than navigation menus. Polite crawling matters too: crawling too fast can overload a small site or get your crawler blocked.`,
        e: `A support bot crawls docs.example.com every night from its sitemap. Of 2,400 pages, the server reports 2,330 unchanged (HTTP 304) and 70 updated; only the 70 are re-parsed and re-embedded, and 6 pages that now return 404 are deleted from the index. A new troubleshooting article published at 5 pm is answerable the next morning. Each indexed chunk keeps its page URL, so the answer's citation opens the live help article, and readers can confirm nothing changed since indexing.`,
        c: `
import requests, trafilatura
r = requests.get("https://docs.example.com/install", timeout=10,
                 headers={"If-None-Match": stored_etag or ""})
if r.status_code == 200:
    text = trafilatura.extract(r.text)        # main article, no menus or ads
    save(url, text, etag=r.headers.get("ETag"))`,
        n: `Check the site's terms and robots.txt, rate-limit politely, and never crawl content behind someone else's login without permission.` },
      { t: `Database Connectors (SQL / NoSQL)`,
        s: `Reading rows or documents from a database and turning them into text records, with their columns kept as metadata.`,
        h: `The connector queries a table or collection, renders each record into readable text with a template (for example "Ticket 4471: printer offline. Resolution: reinstall driver"), uses the primary key as a stable document ID, and stores useful columns (product, status, date, owner) as metadata for filtering. For incremental sync it selects only rows changed since the last run using an updated_at column, or it consumes change events (CDC). Large tables are read in pages to avoid long-running queries.`,
        u: `Use database connectors for support tickets, knowledge-base articles stored in a CMS database, product catalogues, CRM notes, incident records and FAQs kept in tables. Combine them with text-to-SQL when users ask numeric questions ("how many tickets last week?"), because RAG over rendered rows is good at explaining but poor at counting. Read from a replica or with a read-only user so ingestion never affects production performance.`,
        p: `Much valuable knowledge lives in databases, not in files: how past incidents were fixed, product specifications, customer-facing answers. LLMs need it as text to retrieve and quote it. Without a connector, teams export spreadsheets by hand, which are outdated within days. A connector with stable IDs keeps the index in step with the database and lets updates and deletes flow through correctly. Rendering rows into well-labelled sentences also matters: a chunk that says 'Product: ZX-200. Issue: offline. Fix: reinstall driver' retrieves far better than a raw comma-separated row.`,
        e: `Every resolved support ticket (title, description, resolution, product, date) becomes one searchable document with ID "ticket-4471" and filters for product and date. When an agent asks "how did we fix ZX-200 printers going offline?", the assistant retrieves three similar tickets and summarises the fix with links. A ticket edited yesterday was re-ingested last night because its updated_at changed. Deleted tickets disappear from the index at the next sync because the connector compares the full list of IDs weekly.`,
        c: `
rows = db.execute("SELECT id, title, body, resolution, product, updated_at FROM tickets "
                  "WHERE updated_at > %s ORDER BY id LIMIT 1000", [last_sync])
docs = [{"id": f"ticket-{r.id}",
         "text": f"{r.title}\\n{r.body}\\nResolution: {r.resolution}",
         "meta": {"product": r.product, "updated_at": r.updated_at}} for r in rows]`,
        n: `For aggregate questions ("how many", "total", "average") use SQL, not retrieval over text.` },
      { t: `SaaS Connectors (Drive, Confluence, Notion, Slack)`,
        s: `Integrations that pull documents and their permissions from tools like Google Drive, Confluence, Notion or Slack through their APIs.`,
        h: `A connector authenticates with OAuth or a service account, lists items page by page, downloads content in a parseable format (often exporting Google Docs to DOCX or Markdown), and — critically — reads the permissions on each item (users, groups, sharing links). It maps those permissions to your access groups and stores them with the document. For updates it uses the API's change feed or modified timestamps, and it handles rate limits with backoff. Deletions and permission changes must be synced too.`,
        u: `Use SaaS connectors for enterprise search and assistants over the tools where people actually write knowledge: Confluence spaces, Google Drive folders, SharePoint sites, Notion workspaces, Slack channels and Jira. Start with a few high-value spaces owned by a known team, verify permissions carefully, then expand. Many teams buy connectors (or use open-source ones) rather than writing them, because API quirks and permission models are complex.`,
        p: `Knowledge in SaaS tools changes every day, and manual uploads go stale immediately. The bigger risk is permissions: a connector that copies documents but ignores their sharing settings turns the assistant into a way for anyone to read restricted pages. Good connectors keep both content and access rights in sync, so the assistant shows each user only what they could already open in the original tool. API limits and token expiry are the other common failure: a connector that stops silently leaves the index stale for weeks, so connector health must be monitored like any other service.`,
        e: `An engineering assistant syncs three Confluence spaces every 15 minutes. A page restricted to the "Finance" group is indexed with access_groups=["finance"], so an engineer's question never retrieves it. When the page owner removes the restriction, the next sync updates the permission on all of its chunks within 15 minutes, without re-embedding the text. A dashboard shows each connector's last successful sync, so a stalled connector is noticed within the hour.`,
        n: `Permissions are the hard part; test them with real accounts before going live.` },
      { t: `API Ingestion`,
        s: `Getting content by calling an application's API (pull) or by receiving its calls (push), instead of reading files.`,
        h: `In <b>pull</b> mode, a scheduled job calls the source API with pagination and "changed since" parameters, collects new and updated records, and processes them. In <b>push</b> mode, the source system calls your ingestion endpoint or webhook whenever something changes, sending the item or just its ID for you to fetch. Either way you validate the payload, authenticate the caller (API keys, signatures), make processing idempotent so repeated events are harmless, and queue the heavy work for background workers.`,
        u: `Use API ingestion for internal systems without file exports: HR systems, CMS platforms, ticketing tools, product information systems and partner feeds. Push (webhooks) gives near-real-time freshness for fast-changing data; pull is simpler and more robust when the source cannot send events. Many teams combine them: webhooks for speed plus a nightly pull to catch anything a webhook missed. Document the API contract (fields, change semantics, deletes) with the owning team, so schema changes do not break ingestion unnoticed.`,
        p: `Some data has no file export, or changes too often for periodic exports to be useful. Without API ingestion, answers lag behind the source system by days. Webhooks without idempotency or verification, however, create duplicates and security holes, so the design must handle retries, out-of-order events and forged requests. Out-of-order delivery is another trap: an older update arriving after a newer one must not overwrite it, so compare version numbers or timestamps before applying changes.`,
        e: `The CMS sends a signed webhook "article 812 published". The ingestion service verifies the signature, enqueues a job, fetches the article through the CMS API, and indexes it within a minute. If the CMS retries the webhook because of a timeout, the job sees article 812 at the same version and does nothing, thanks to idempotent processing. A nightly pull compares all article IDs with the index and repairs anything the webhooks missed, such as articles deleted while the endpoint was down.` },
      { t: `Structured vs Unstructured Data`,
        s: `Structured data has fixed fields (tables, JSON records); unstructured data is free text, PDFs, images and audio.`,
        h: `Unstructured data goes through parsing, chunking and embedding so it can be searched by meaning. Structured data is best queried directly (SQL, filters, aggregations), or rendered into text with its fields kept as metadata when it needs to be searched alongside documents. Semi-structured data (HTML, emails, JSON with text fields) is in between: extract the text fields for search and keep the rest as metadata. Choosing the right path per data type is an architecture decision, not a tuning detail.`,
        u: `Decide the path per source: RAG for documents, policies and articles; SQL or text-to-SQL for numbers, lists and aggregations; both for hybrid questions that need a figure and an explanation. Keep structured attributes (dates, owners, products, prices) as metadata on unstructured documents to enable precise filters. Many enterprise questions mix both, so plan a router or an agent that can call SQL and retrieval and combine the results with citations for each part.`,
        p: `Forcing tables through chunking and embedding loses exact numbers and makes counting impossible; forcing documents into rigid tables loses their meaning. Each kind of data needs its own treatment. Mixing them up is a common cause of wrong numeric answers in RAG systems. It also affects freshness and permissions: structured data usually has row-level security in the database, which should be used directly rather than copied into a document index.`,
        e: `"What does the leave policy say about carry-over?" → RAG over the policy PDF. "How many leave days does Ravi have left this year?" → SQL over the HR database, permission-checked. "Can I carry over my remaining days?" → both: SQL for Ravi's balance, RAG for the carry-over rule, combined into one cited answer. Each part of the combined answer cites its own source: the HR database for the balance and the policy page for the rule.` },
      { t: `Batch Ingestion`,
        s: `Processing many documents together on a schedule (for example every night) rather than one at a time as they change.`,
        h: `A batch job collects everything new or changed since its last checkpoint, splits it into work items, processes them in parallel workers (parse, chunk, embed, index), records progress so it can resume after a failure, and finally advances the checkpoint. Throughput is high because work is batched (embedding 64 chunks at once, bulk index writes), and failures are retried per item without restarting everything. Progress is tracked per item, so the job knows exactly what is left after any interruption.`,
        u: `Use batch ingestion for the initial bulk load of an archive, nightly re-syncs of sources without change notifications, periodic full reconciliation, and re-processing after a pipeline change (new chunking or embedding model). Run heavy batches off-peak, and write them into a new index generation when re-processing everything, so live search is not disturbed. Track per-batch metrics — items processed, failed, skipped, duration — so regressions in throughput or failure rate are visible.`,
        p: `Loading 100,000 documents one request at a time would take days and could overload the source system and the indexes. Batching uses resources efficiently, is easy to monitor and retry, and gives predictable load. Its limitation is freshness: data is only as current as the last batch, so fast-changing sources need streaming as well. Batches also need care with resources: a huge batch can saturate the embedding GPUs or the search cluster, so throttle it or run it in a separate index generation while live traffic continues.`,
        e: `A law firm imports two million archived contracts over a weekend: 32 workers parse and OCR in parallel, embeddings are computed in batches of 64 on two GPUs, and bulk writes go into a new index. A crash on Saturday night resumes from the last checkpoint instead of starting again, and the new index is validated and published on Monday morning. Throughout the weekend, the old index kept answering questions, so users never noticed the import.` },
      { t: `Streaming Ingestion (Kafka, Webhooks)`,
        s: `Processing each change as it happens, from an event stream or webhook, instead of waiting for the next batch.`,
        h: `Source systems publish events ("document 42 updated", "page 9 deleted") to a stream such as Kafka or a queue such as SQS, or call a webhook. Consumers read events, fetch or receive the content, and update the indexes. Events for the same document must be processed in order (partition by document ID), retries need backoff, and events that keep failing go to a dead-letter queue for inspection. Freshness drops from hours to seconds. Exactly-once delivery is rare, so consumers are written to tolerate duplicates.`,
        u: `Use streaming for fast-changing knowledge: support tickets, incident updates, chat-based knowledge, news, prices and inventory. Combine it with a periodic reconciliation batch, because events can be lost or arrive out of order. Monitor consumer lag (how far behind the stream you are) as a key freshness metric. Keep events small (IDs and change type) and fetch the full content when processing, so large payloads do not clog the stream.`,
        p: `With nightly batches, a question about something that changed an hour ago gets an outdated answer — unacceptable during an incident or for live prices. Streaming makes changes searchable within seconds. Without ordering and idempotency, though, an older update can overwrite a newer one, so the consumer design matters as much as the stream itself. Streaming also adds operational work: brokers, consumer groups, schemas and monitoring. Use it where freshness truly matters and keep batch sync for slow-changing sources.`,
        e: `An on-call assistant consumes incident events from Kafka. When the incident commander posts "Workaround: restart the payment gateway with flag --safe", it is indexed within ten seconds, so the next engineer asking "what is the current workaround for INC-77?" gets the new instruction instead of yesterday's. Consumer lag is shown on the on-call dashboard; if it grows beyond a minute, an alert fires before answers become stale. Without streaming, the same question would have returned yesterday's workaround.` },
      { t: `Pipeline Orchestration (Airflow, Prefect)`,
        s: `A scheduler that runs multi-step data pipelines in the right order, retries failures and shows what ran.`,
        h: `You define a graph of tasks with dependencies — extract from sources → parse → chunk → embed → index → validate → evaluate — plus schedules, retry rules and timeouts. The orchestrator (Airflow, Prefect, Dagster) runs tasks when their inputs are ready, records each run's state and logs, alerts on failures, and lets you re-run only the failed parts. For per-document work inside a run, task queues such as Celery handle the fan-out.`,
        u: `Use an orchestrator when ingestion involves several sources and steps on schedules, when re-indexing and evaluation must run in sequence, or when several teams need visibility into pipeline runs. Small systems can start with a task queue and cron, then move to an orchestrator as pipelines grow. Keep tasks small and idempotent so any one can be re-run safely, and pass references (IDs, paths) between tasks rather than large data.`,
        p: `Cron scripts fail silently, run out of order, cannot resume halfway, and leave no history of what happened. That leads to stale or half-updated indexes nobody notices. An orchestrator makes pipelines visible, restartable and alertable, which is essential once several sources feed the same index. Orchestration also documents the pipeline: the DAG itself shows new team members what runs, in which order and how often, which is hard to learn from scattered scripts.`,
        e: `An Airflow DAG starts at 01:00: sync three sources in parallel, then rebuild the index generation, then run the evaluation suite, then publish only if the gates pass. When the Confluence sync fails because of an expired token, the DAG stops before publishing and alerts the team; after the token is renewed, they re-run just the failed task. The run history shows that the failure happened only once in three months, which helped the team decide that a token-expiry alert was enough.` },
      { t: `Idempotent Ingestion`,
        s: `Running the same ingestion twice gives the same result — no duplicates and no corruption.`,
        h: `Idempotency comes from three habits. Deterministic IDs: each chunk's ID is computed from the document version, its position and a hash of its content, so re-processing produces the same IDs. Upserts instead of inserts: writing an existing ID replaces it rather than adding a copy. "Skip if unchanged" checks: a content hash compared with the stored one avoids repeating work. Together they make retries after crashes, timeouts or duplicate events completely safe.`,
        u: `Apply it to every ingestion pipeline — jobs will be retried: workers crash, deploys restart them, webhooks are delivered twice, and queues redeliver messages. It is also what makes re-running a failed batch or a whole re-index a routine operation rather than a risky one. Test it deliberately: run the same job twice in staging and check that chunk counts and index sizes do not change. It applies equally to deletes and permission updates, which also get replayed.`,
        p: `Without idempotency, a retried job doubles every chunk of a document. Search then returns each passage twice, crowding out other evidence, counts and evaluation metrics become wrong, and deletes miss the duplicate copies. These bugs are hard to notice because nothing errors — results just get gradually worse. Idempotency also makes operations calm: when something fails at 2 a.m., the on-call engineer can simply re-run the job without worrying about side effects or manual clean-up.`,
        e: `A worker dies halfway through embedding a 300-page PDF. The queue redelivers the job; the retry produces exactly the same chunk IDs (uuid5 of version, index and content hash) and upserts them, overwriting the half-written ones. The index ends with exactly one copy of each chunk, and nobody has to clean anything up. The same design lets a full re-index run on top of the old data without creating any duplicates. Chunk counts before and after the retry are identical, which is the simplest check that idempotency works.`,
        c: `
import hashlib, uuid
NS = uuid.UUID("6f1c0d7e-0000-4000-8000-000000000000")
def chunk_id(doc_version_id, index, text):
    h = hashlib.sha256(text.encode()).hexdigest()
    return uuid.uuid5(NS, f"{doc_version_id}:{index}:{h}")   # same input -> same ID
vector_store.upsert(id=str(chunk_id(v, 0, text)), vector=vec)  # upsert, never insert` },
    ]},
    { id: "3.2", title: "Sync & Change Management", items: [
      { t: `Incremental Sync`,
        s: `Fetching and processing only what changed since the last sync, instead of everything.`,
        h: `The connector keeps a checkpoint (last sync time, a change-feed cursor or ETags). Each run asks the source for items modified after the checkpoint, compares each item's content hash with the stored one to skip cosmetic updates, processes truly new or changed items, applies deletions reported by the source, and then advances the checkpoint. Because change feeds can miss events, a slower full reconciliation runs occasionally (e.g. weekly) to compare the full list of IDs and fix drift.`,
        u: `Use incremental sync for every connector after the initial full load: file shares, SaaS tools, databases and websites. Choose the change signal the source supports best (change feed, modified timestamp, ETag, CDC), and store checkpoints per source so one failing source does not block others. Record the checkpoint only after a run succeeds, so a crashed run is simply repeated. Measure how many items each run processes; a sudden drop to zero can mean the checkpoint is stuck.`,
        p: `Re-processing a whole corpus every night wastes hours of compute and API quota, and large corpora cannot be fully reprocessed fast enough to stay fresh. Incremental sync makes freshness affordable. Without the periodic reconcile, though, missed events slowly accumulate into stale or orphaned content. Incremental sync also reduces load on the source systems and on API quotas, which matters for SaaS tools that throttle heavy users. It is usually the difference between a sync that finishes in minutes and one that never catches up.`,
        e: `Of 50,000 Drive files, 120 changed today. Only those 120 are downloaded; 15 of them have identical content hashes (only sharing metadata changed), so only their permissions are updated, and 105 are re-parsed and re-embedded. The sync finishes in four minutes instead of six hours. The weekly reconcile later found two files whose change events were never sent and fixed them automatically. Users see the edited files in answers within minutes of the change.`,
        c: `
since = state.get("last_sync")
for item in source.list(modified_after=since):
    if content_hash(item) != stored_hash(item.id):
        reindex(item)
    else:
        update_permissions(item)          # metadata-only change
state["last_sync"] = run_started_at` },
      { t: `Change Data Capture (CDC)`,
        s: `Reading a database's own change log to receive every insert, update and delete as an event.`,
        h: `Databases record every change in a log (Postgres write-ahead log, MySQL binlog) for durability and replication. CDC tools such as Debezium read that log and publish one event per row change — with the before and after values — to Kafka. Consumers update the RAG index from these events. There is no polling, deletes are captured, and the load on the database is minimal because the log is read, not queried. Each event carries a position in the log, so consumers can resume exactly where they stopped.`,
        u: `Use CDC to keep a RAG index in sync with operational databases in near real time: product catalogues, ticket systems, knowledge articles stored in a database, pricing tables. It is especially useful when rows are hard-deleted, because timestamp polling cannot see deletions. Filter events to the tables and columns that matter for search, so irrelevant updates do not trigger re-embedding. Managed CDC services exist for most cloud databases if running Debezium yourself is too much.`,
        p: `Polling with "updated_at > last_run" misses hard deletes, depends on every application setting the timestamp correctly, and adds query load to production databases. CDC captures every change reliably, in order, with low overhead. The trade-off is operational complexity (Kafka, connectors), which is worth it for important, fast-changing data. It also preserves order and history, so consumers can rebuild derived data if needed by replaying the stream from a point in time.`,
        e: `A product description is edited in Postgres at 10:02. Debezium emits an update event at 10:02; a consumer re-renders and re-embeds that product's chunk by 10:03. When a discontinued product's row is deleted, the delete event removes its chunks from both search indexes immediately, so the assistant stops recommending it. Because events carry the before and after values, the consumer skips updates that only touched columns not used in search.` },
      { t: `Content Hashing`,
        s: `A fingerprint (for example SHA-256) of a file's or chunk's content — the same content always gives the same hash.`,
        h: `Compute a cryptographic hash of the raw bytes for files, or of normalised text for chunks (trimmed, consistent whitespace). Store it with the document version and each chunk. Equal hashes mean identical content, so work can be skipped; a different hash for the same document ID means a new version. Hashes also make good building blocks for deterministic IDs and cache keys. SHA-256 is the usual choice; faster non-cryptographic hashes are fine for caches but not for integrity checks.`,
        u: `Use content hashes to detect duplicate uploads, decide whether a synced item really changed, build embedding caches (hash + model version → vector), generate idempotent chunk IDs, and verify that stored files were not corrupted (re-hash on read). Hash normalised text (not raw bytes) for chunks, so whitespace-only changes do not count as new content. Store the hash next to every document version and chunk, and index it so lookups are fast.`,
        p: `Without hashes, the system re-processes unchanged files on every sync, wastes embedding compute, and stores duplicate uploads as separate documents that then compete in search results. Hashes make change detection exact and cheap. Hashes also protect integrity: re-hashing a stored file before processing detects corruption or tampering. They are cheap to compute and store, so there is no reason not to use them everywhere.`,
        e: `The same Employee Handbook is uploaded twice by two HR staff. Its SHA-256 matches the stored version, so the second upload is recognised as a duplicate and linked to the existing document instead of creating 50 duplicate chunks. When HR edits one paragraph and uploads again, the hash differs and version 2 is created. The chunk-level hashes also showed that only 3 of the 50 chunks changed, so only those 3 were re-embedded.`,
        c: `
import hashlib
def sha256_file(path):
    h = hashlib.sha256()
    with open(path, "rb") as f:
        for block in iter(lambda: f.read(65536), b""):
            h.update(block)
    return h.hexdigest()` },
      { t: `Exact Deduplication`,
        s: `Removing items whose content is exactly the same, keeping one copy.`,
        h: `Normalise lightly (trim, collapse whitespace, maybe lowercase), hash, and keep one item per hash — optionally recording all the places it came from. It works at file level (same document uploaded twice) and at chunk level (identical disclaimers, headers or boilerplate repeated across many documents). It is cheap, precise and has no false positives. Keep a record of every source location for a deduplicated item, so deleting one source does not remove content that another source still provides.`,
        u: `Apply it during ingestion for duplicate uploads, mirrored folders, email attachments sent many times, and repeated boilerplate chunks such as confidentiality notices and standard footers. Apply it again when assembling context, so the same passage is not sent to the LLM twice. Report deduplication statistics per source; a sudden jump often means a connector is re-sending the same files. It is the cheapest quality improvement in the whole pipeline.`,
        p: `Duplicates waste storage and embedding compute, but the bigger problem is retrieval: five identical chunks can fill all top-5 slots, pushing out other evidence the answer needs. Deduplication keeps result lists diverse and useful. It also saves cost at scale: deduplicating before embedding avoids paying to embed and store the same text many times. Deduplication must still respect permissions: two identical files in different access groups should keep both access lists on the single stored copy.`,
        e: `A legal disclaimer repeated at the bottom of 400 contract pages becomes one chunk instead of 400. Before deduplication, a question about "limitation of liability" returned the disclaimer five times; afterwards it returned the disclaimer once plus four relevant contract clauses. Context assembly also deduplicates, so even if two chunks with the same text are retrieved, the LLM sees it only once. The storage and embedding work also dropped by the same 399 chunks.` },
      { t: `Near-duplicate Detection (MinHash / SimHash)`,
        s: `Finding documents that are almost the same — a few words changed — without comparing every pair.`,
        h: `MinHash splits text into overlapping word sequences (shingles) and keeps a short signature of the minimum hash values under many hash functions; the share of matching signature positions estimates the Jaccard similarity of the shingle sets. Locality-sensitive hashing (LSH) groups signatures into buckets so only likely-similar pairs are compared. SimHash builds one 64-bit fingerprint where similar texts differ in only a few bits. Both scale to millions of documents.`,
        u: `Use it for large web crawls (the same article on several URLs), document templates with small edits (offer letters, contracts), versions kept as separate files, forwarded email threads and copied policies. Decide a policy for each cluster: keep the newest, the most authoritative, or all with a pointer to a canonical one. Tune the similarity threshold on samples: too low merges genuinely different documents, too high misses real near-duplicates.`,
        p: `Exact hashing misses documents that differ by a date or a name, so twenty near-identical versions of a template still flood the results and confuse the LLM with slightly different numbers. Near-duplicate detection groups them so only the right one is indexed or shown. It also helps quality reviews: clusters of near-duplicates often reveal outdated copies that content owners should retire. It also reduces cost, because near-identical documents are not embedded and indexed many times.`,
        e: `"Offer letter template v3" and "v4" differ in two lines; MinHash estimates 95% similarity and flags them. The ingestion policy keeps only v4 (the current one) in the search index and marks v3 as superseded, so answers about notice periods come from the current template. A cluster report also showed HR 14 old copies of the template saved in personal folders, which they cleaned up. Answers about notice periods became consistent, because only one version of the clause was retrievable.`,
        c: `
from datasketch import MinHash, MinHashLSH
def sig(text):
    m = MinHash(num_perm=128)
    for w in set(text.lower().split()):
        m.update(w.encode())
    return m
lsh = MinHashLSH(threshold=0.8, num_perm=128)
lsh.insert("v3", sig(template_v3))
print(lsh.query(sig(template_v4)))   # ['v3'] -> near-duplicate` },
      { t: `Document IDs & Lineage`,
        s: `A stable ID for every document and a record of where each chunk came from — source, version, parser and pipeline.`,
        h: `Each source item gets a stable ID, typically source system + its native ID ("confluence:8812"). Every version has its own ID, and every chunk references the document version, its position, and the pipeline version that produced it (which parser, chunker and embedding model). Lineage is stored in the database, so any chunk — and any answer citing it — can be traced back to the exact file version and processing steps. Lineage records are written in the same transaction as the chunks, so they never fall out of step.`,
        u: `Use lineage for citations that open the exact version, for deletes ("remove everything derived from file X"), for debugging bad answers, for audits ("which documents supported this answer on 3 May?"), and for targeted re-processing ("re-parse only documents processed by parser 1.x"). Expose lineage in admin tools so support and content teams can answer 'where did this come from?' without engineers.`,
        p: `Without stable IDs and lineage you cannot reliably delete a document's chunks from every index, explain where an answer came from, or re-process only what a bug affected. In regulated environments, missing lineage can make the system unauditable. Lineage also enables safe experiments: comparing two pipeline versions requires knowing exactly which chunks each produced. It also makes cost and quality analysis possible per source and per pipeline version.`,
        e: `A chunk record says: Confluence page 8812, version 14, parsed by Docling 2.x, chunked by structure_aware v2, embedded with e5-small, in index generation 2. When users report a wrong answer, engineers see the chunk came from an outdated version 13 that was never retired — and fix the sync bug. Fixing the sync bug and re-processing only the affected documents took minutes, because lineage showed exactly which ones they were.` },
      { t: `Document Versioning`,
        s: `Keeping each change of a document as a new version instead of overwriting the old one.`,
        h: `When content changes (different hash), create version n+1, process it fully (parse, chunk, embed, index), then switch the "current version" pointer and retire the previous version's chunks from search. Old versions stay stored for audit and comparison, often with effective dates. Search normally uses only current versions; historical questions can explicitly target older ones. Versions share the same document ID, so links and permissions stay stable across updates.`,
        u: `Use versioning for policies, contracts, specifications, procedures and anything where "what did it say on date X?" matters. It also enables safe updates: the new version is indexed before the old one is removed, so there is never a moment when the document is missing. Show the version and its date in citations, so users know which version an answer is based on. Keep the number of live versions per document at one unless historical search is a requirement.`,
        p: `Overwriting loses history and makes audits impossible. Keeping several versions live without clear rules makes the LLM quote old and new rules together, producing contradictory answers. Versioning with a single current pointer solves both. It also enables comparisons: questions like 'what changed in the leave policy this year?' can be answered by retrieving both versions explicitly. Versioning also makes rollbacks of content possible: if a new version was uploaded by mistake, the previous one can be made current again in seconds.`,
        e: `Leave policy v3 (effective April 2026) replaces v2. v3 is processed and indexed; then v2's chunks are removed from the live index. Answers now say "24 days", while an auditor can still open v2 and see it said "20 days" until March 2026. A question 'what changed in the leave policy?' retrieves both versions and the answer lists the difference with both citations. If HR later finds an error in v3, they can make v2 current again while v4 is prepared.` },
      { t: `Soft vs Hard Deletes`,
        s: `A soft delete marks an item as deleted (hidden, recoverable); a hard delete physically removes it everywhere.`,
        h: `Soft delete sets a deleted_at timestamp, filters the item out of every query, and removes its chunks from search indexes, while keeping the record for recovery and audit. Hard delete removes the data from the database, object storage, every search index, caches, logs that copied it, and eventually backups (by retention). Systems often soft-delete immediately and hard-delete later by a scheduled purge, or immediately when the law requires it.`,
        u: `Use soft deletes for normal user deletions (mistakes can be undone) and audit trails. Use hard deletes for privacy requests ("right to be forgotten"), legal holds that end, retention expiry and leaked confidential content. Make sure both paths reach every copy, including vector and keyword indexes. Write tests that delete a document and then search for its text, to prove every path works. Document which data is soft-deleted, which is hard-deleted, and how long each step takes, so users and auditors know what to expect.`,
        p: `The classic RAG bug: a document is "deleted" in the app but its chunks remain in the vector index or a cache, so the assistant keeps quoting it. That is both a correctness and a privacy failure. A clear delete design, plus a reconciliation job that removes orphans, prevents it. Deletes are also easy to forget in caches: semantic answer caches and retrieval caches must be cleared or keyed by index version, otherwise a cached answer can keep quoting deleted content.`,
        e: `In this platform, deleting a document hides it immediately, marks it DELETED, removes its chunks from both Qdrant and OpenSearch through an index.delete event, and purges its files from MinIO. A nightly reconcile job removes any index entries whose chunk no longer exists, catching anything a failed event missed. An automated test deletes a sample document and confirms that no search, cache or citation still returns it. Deleted documents can still be restored by an admin for a short period, after which the purge removes them for good.` },
      { t: `Data Freshness & TTL`,
        s: `Freshness is how up to date the index is; TTL (time to live) automatically expires data after a set time.`,
        h: `Measure freshness as the delay between a change in the source and the change being searchable, per source, and set a target (for example 15 minutes for tickets, 24 hours for policies). Monitor it with probes and connector lag. TTL fields let indexes, caches or documents expire automatically: announcements after their end date, cached answers after an hour, news after 90 days. Freshness targets should differ per source, because a price list and an archive have very different needs.`,
        u: `Use freshness targets for every source and alert when they are missed. Use TTL for time-limited content (offers, notices, events), for caches (so they never serve stale answers for long), and for data that legally must not be kept beyond a period. Show the indexed date on citations so users can judge freshness themselves. Review TTLs regularly, since content owners often extend events or offers without updating the expiry date.`,
        p: `Stale answers are a quiet failure: "the office is closed on Friday" from last month's notice, or a price that changed yesterday. Users lose trust quickly. Freshness monitoring catches broken syncs early, and TTL removes content that should no longer be shown without anyone having to remember to delete it. Freshness also needs an owner: when a source falls behind its target, someone must be alerted and responsible for fixing it.`,
        e: `Office announcements carry an expires_at date; after it passes they are excluded from retrieval. A freshness probe edits a test page every 10 minutes and alerts if search still shows the old text after 20 minutes — which caught an expired Confluence token within half an hour. After the token was renewed, the probe confirmed freshness was back within target. The freshness dashboard now shows each source's current lag next to its target, so problems are visible before users notice them.` },
      { t: `Re-ingestion Scheduling`,
        s: `Planning when to re-process content: after source changes, parser or model upgrades, or as periodic maintenance.`,
        h: `There are three triggers. Content changes are handled by incremental sync. Pipeline changes (a better parser, new chunking, a new embedding model, contextual headers) require re-processing affected or all documents — done into a new index generation, validated, then swapped in. Periodic full reconciliation catches drift. Heavy re-processing is scheduled off-peak, prioritised (most-used documents first), and tracked like any other release.`,
        u: `Use it whenever the pipeline improves, when you discover a parsing bug affecting a document type, and as routine maintenance (e.g. monthly reconcile). Track which pipeline version processed each document so you can target re-processing precisely. Estimate re-processing time and cost before starting: OCR and embedding of a large archive can take days on CPU. Prioritise documents that are retrieved most often, so the biggest share of answers improves first.`,
        p: `Teams deploy a better parser but old documents keep their bad parses forever, because nothing triggers re-processing. Or they re-process everything in place during working hours and degrade live search. Planned re-ingestion into a separate index avoids both. It also needs evaluation: a re-processed index can be better on average but worse for some document types, so compare it with the live index on the golden set before switching.`,
        e: `After switching to contextual chunk headers, this platform rebuilt all chunks with pipeline v2 into a new index generation while v1 kept serving. The new generation was validated (7 checks), evaluated against v1, approved, and then made live in one step. The old generation stayed available for rollback for two weeks after the switch. Re-ingestion was tracked like a release, with its own evaluation run and approval record.` },
    ]},
    { id: "3.3", title: "Data Quality & Governance", items: [
      { t: `Metadata Extraction`,
        s: `Pulling facts about a document — title, author, dates, type, department, language — from the file, the source system or the text itself.`,
        h: `Metadata comes from four places: file properties (PDF and DOCX metadata such as title and author), the source system (Confluence space, Drive owner, ticket status), the path or URL (/hr/policies/2025/), and the content (dates, document type, product names extracted with regex, NER or a small LLM). Extracted values are normalised to your schema and stored on the document, then copied to every chunk. Source-system metadata is usually the most reliable, extracted-from-text metadata the least.`,
        u: `Use metadata for filters ("only 2025 HR policies"), citations ("Leave Policy, HR, updated 2025-04-01"), routing (send IT questions to IT documents), ranking boosts (prefer official or recent documents) and reporting (coverage by department). Validate extracted values (dates parse, departments are in the allowed list) and log failures for review. Prefer the source system's values when available and fall back to extraction only when needed.`,
        p: `Without metadata, users cannot narrow results, the system cannot prefer current or authoritative sources, and citations say only "document.pdf". Good metadata makes retrieval both more precise and more explainable. Metadata errors are quiet but harmful: a wrong year silently hides a document from date-filtered questions. Validation and spot checks keep it reliable. It also powers analytics: knowing which departments' documents are used most guides content investment.`,
        e: `A file stored at /hr/policies/2025/leave.pdf gets department=HR, type=policy, year=2025 from its path, title "Leave Policy" from its PDF properties, and effective_from=2025-04-01 extracted from its first page. The question "what is the 2025 leave policy?" can now filter precisely instead of searching everything. A validation rule caught 40 documents whose year came out as 1900 because of a date-format bug, before they were indexed.` },
      { t: `Metadata Schema Design`,
        s: `Deciding in advance which metadata fields every document and chunk must have, with types and allowed values.`,
        h: `Define required fields (doc_id, version, title, source, created_at, updated_at, access_groups, language) and optional ones (department, doc_type, product, effective dates, tags). Use fixed vocabularies for fields used in filters, typed values (dates as dates, not text), and clear ownership of each field. Index the filterable fields in both the vector store and the search engine, and validate incoming metadata against the schema. Version the schema itself, so changes are deliberate and documented.`,
        u: `Do it before building connectors, because changing the schema later means re-indexing everything. Revisit it when adding new sources, and keep a mapping per source from its native labels to your schema. Keep the number of required fields small; too many required fields lead to placeholder values that are worse than missing ones. Involve content owners and security teams when defining access and classification fields.`,
        p: `When "dept", "department" and "Department" mean the same thing in different sources, filters silently miss documents. When dates are free text, date filters cannot work. A schema makes metadata consistent, filterable and trustworthy across sources. A schema also enables governance: required access fields can be enforced at ingestion, so no document is indexed without permissions. It also makes onboarding new sources faster, because every connector knows exactly what it must provide.`,
        e: `The schema says doc_type ∈ {policy, contract, guide, faq} and department ∈ {HR, IT, Finance, Legal}. The SharePoint connector maps "Human Resources" → HR and "Policies & Procedures" → policy, so one filter works across all sources. The schema rejects documents without access_groups, so nothing can be indexed without a permission decision. Adding a fourth source later took a day of mapping work instead of a re-design.` },
      { t: `Access Control (ACL) Propagation`,
        s: `Copying who may read each document from the source into every chunk in every index, and keeping it in sync.`,
        h: `Read permissions from the source (users, groups, roles, sharing links), normalise them to your access groups, store them on the document, and copy them onto every chunk as a filterable field in the vector store and the keyword index. When permissions change, update that field on all chunks — usually without re-embedding. At query time the signed-in user's groups become a mandatory filter inside every search, and results are re-checked before display.`,
        u: `Apply it in every multi-user or enterprise RAG system, and test it with real accounts from different groups. Include permission changes in sync, not only content changes. Log permission changes and their propagation time, so you can prove when access was granted or removed. Permissions should cover citations, previews and downloads too, not just retrieval. Large organisations also need group nesting resolved correctly, since many permissions are granted to groups of groups.`,
        p: `The biggest real-world RAG risk is an assistant that answers from documents the user is not allowed to see — salary sheets, investigation reports, board minutes. Filtering after retrieval in application code is fragile; propagating ACLs into the indexes and filtering inside the search is the reliable approach. It also has to be fast: when someone loses access, every index must reflect it within minutes, otherwise the system leaks data during that window.`,
        e: `A Drive folder shared only with "Finance" → its chunks carry access_groups=["finance"]. An engineer asking about salary bands gets "I could not find this", because the filter excludes those chunks inside both Qdrant and OpenSearch. When Finance shares the folder with "managers", the next sync updates the field and managers can retrieve it. Permission-only updates take seconds because the vectors stay the same; only the access field on each chunk changes.`,
        c: `
# indexing: every chunk carries the document's groups
point = {"id": cid, "vector": vec, "payload": {"doc_id": d.id, "access_groups": d.groups}}
# querying: the filter comes from the signed-in session, never from the prompt
flt = {"must": [{"key": "access_groups", "match": {"any": user.groups}}]}
hits = qdrant.query_points("chunks", query=qv, query_filter=flt, limit=20).points` },
      { t: `PII Detection & Redaction`,
        s: `Finding personal data — names, emails, phone numbers, ID numbers, bank details — and masking or removing it before it spreads into indexes and logs.`,
        h: `Detection combines pattern rules (emails, phone formats, card numbers with checksum validation, national ID formats) with NER models for names, addresses and organisations — for example Microsoft Presidio. Then a policy decides per field and source: mask ("[EMAIL]"), pseudonymise (consistent fake values), keep but restrict access, or exclude the document. Apply it before indexing and before writing logs, traces and evaluation datasets. Detection is never perfect, so combine it with access control rather than relying on redaction alone.`,
        u: `Use PII handling for support tickets, HR documents, medical or financial records, emails and chat transcripts — and for any system whose logs or evaluation sets copy user questions. Agree the policy with the data owner and legal team. Measure detection quality on a labelled sample; missed PII and over-masking both have costs. Apply the same rules to text extracted by OCR, which often contains forms full of personal data.`,
        p: `Indexes, caches, logs and test datasets are all copies of the data. Personal data in them multiplies the impact of a breach and complicates deletion requests. Detecting and handling PII at ingestion keeps sensitive data where it belongs. It also simplifies compliance: data that was never copied into indexes and logs does not need to be found and deleted later. Redaction also needs an audit trail, so you can show which fields were masked and under which policy.`,
        e: `Support tickets are indexed with customer phone numbers and emails masked, so the bot can explain how similar issues were fixed without exposing anyone's contact details. Logged user questions are scrubbed the same way before storage. Masking is consistent, so the same customer in two tickets gets the same placeholder, which keeps the tickets readable. Engineers debugging search see masked logs, so they can work without access to customers' personal details.`,
        c: `
from presidio_analyzer import AnalyzerEngine
from presidio_anonymizer import AnonymizerEngine
text = "Call Ravi at +91 98450 12345 or ravi@example.com"
found = AnalyzerEngine().analyze(text=text, language="en")
print(AnonymizerEngine().anonymize(text=text, analyzer_results=found).text)
# Call <PERSON> at <PHONE_NUMBER> or <EMAIL_ADDRESS>` },
      { t: `Data Quality Filtering`,
        s: `Dropping or quarantining content that would hurt answers: empty pages, garbled OCR, boilerplate, spam and auto-generated noise.`,
        h: `Apply measurable checks: minimum text length, share of real dictionary words, OCR confidence, language detection, ratio of links to text (navigation pages), duplicate ratio, and file-type sanity. Items that fail are logged and moved to a review queue rather than silently dropped, so owners can fix the source. Thresholds are tuned on samples per document type. Each rule records why an item was filtered, so reviewers can act quickly.`,
        u: `Use quality filtering on web crawls, scanned archives, user-generated content, email imports and any source you do not control. It is also useful after parser upgrades to catch new failure patterns. Report filtered items per source so owners see and fix systemic problems. Apply the filters again after parser upgrades, because new parsers can produce new kinds of junk. Keep thresholds per document type, since scanned forms and clean reports behave very differently.`,
        p: `Garbage chunks often rank surprisingly high — garbled OCR can match many queries by accident — and make the LLM quote nonsense. Filtering protects answer quality, while the review queue makes sure real documents are not lost. It also saves cost: not embedding and storing junk content reduces index size and speeds up search. Filtering also makes evaluation cleaner, because junk chunks no longer distract the retriever or the reranker.`,
        e: `Pages with average OCR confidence below 60% are held for review instead of being indexed; a reviewer finds that one scanner produced low-resolution images and re-scans 300 pages at 300 DPI, after which they pass and become searchable. Navigation-only pages from the crawl were filtered too, removing hundreds of chunks that only contained menus. After the re-scan, questions about those 300 pages started getting correct, cited answers.` },
      { t: `Source Authority / Trust Level`,
        s: `Marking how trustworthy each source is — official policy, team wiki, personal notes, chat messages — and using it in ranking and answers.`,
        h: `Assign a trust level per source or document type (for example official_policy = 1.0, wiki = 0.6, chat = 0.3) and store it as metadata. Use it to boost ranking, break ties, filter ("official sources only" for compliance questions), and inform the LLM which source wins when sources disagree. Show it in citations so users can judge the evidence. Trust levels should be assigned by policy, not by the model.`,
        u: `Use trust levels in mixed corpora that combine official documentation with wikis, Slack threads, emails or forum posts, and for questions where only authoritative answers are acceptable (HR rules, legal, finance). Review trust assignments with content owners; they know which sources are authoritative. Combine trust with recency carefully: a recent informal message should not outrank a current official policy. Make trust visible in the interface so users understand why a source was preferred.`,
        p: `Without trust levels, a two-year-old Slack message ("I think we get 20 days") can outrank the official policy and produce a wrong answer. Authority-aware ranking makes the official source win when it exists. It also helps users: citations labelled 'Official policy' versus 'Team chat' let them judge how much to rely on the answer. It also supports governance: compliance teams can require that certain answers cite only high-trust sources.`,
        e: `For "how many leave days?", the HR policy (trust 1.0) and a Slack thread (trust 0.3) both match. The reranking boost puts the policy first, and the prompt tells the model to follow official policy if sources conflict, so the answer is "24 days [1]". The Slack thread is still available as a secondary source, labelled as informal. Compliance questions are restricted to sources with trust 1.0, so informal sources are never cited for them.` },
      { t: `Knowledge Base Curation & Ownership`,
        s: `Assigning owners who keep each part of the knowledge base correct, current and free of contradictions.`,
        h: `Each collection or source has an owner team responsible for its content. They review content flagged by users or by the system, retire outdated documents, resolve contradictions, approve what is indexed, and fill gaps that the assistant reveals. The RAG platform supports them with reports: unanswered questions by topic, documents never retrieved, low-rated answers by source. Ownership is recorded as metadata, so every document has a named team to contact.`,
        u: `Set up ownership for any knowledge base that lives longer than a pilot. Include curation in the operating model alongside engineering — RAG quality depends on content quality as much as on code. Give owners simple tools: a list of their documents, how often each is used, and reported problems. Review ownership whenever teams reorganise, so no collection becomes orphaned.`,
        p: `RAG faithfully repeats whatever the documents say. Outdated, duplicated or contradictory content produces wrong answers that no model or prompt can fix. Clear ownership turns content problems into actionable tasks for the right people. It also builds trust between teams: content owners see the assistant as a tool that improves their documents, not a black box that misquotes them.`,
        e: `HR receives a weekly report: "37 questions about parental leave were not answered; 2 leave documents contradict each other on carry-over". They publish the missing parental-leave policy and retire the outdated FAQ, and next week's unanswered count for that topic drops to zero. The same report also lists documents nobody has retrieved in six months, candidates for retirement. The team tracks unanswered questions by topic over time, which shows the curation work paying off.` },
      { t: `Temporal Validity (Effective Dates)`,
        s: `Recording when each document is valid (effective from and until), so the right version answers time-specific questions.`,
        h: `Store effective_from and effective_to (or valid-from dates per version) as indexed metadata. By default, retrieval filters to documents valid today. When a question mentions a date or year ("in 2024", "last quarter"), the filter targets that period instead. Citations show the effective date so users see which version applies. Documents without dates are treated as valid until replaced, which keeps simple cases simple.`,
        u: `Use it for policies, tax rules, price lists, contracts, regulations, benefit plans and procedures — anything where the correct answer depends on the date. Make 'currently valid' the default filter and require an explicit date for historical questions. Include effective dates in the source labels given to the LLM, so it can mention which period an answer covers.`,
        p: `Without validity dates, "How many leave days did I get in 2024?" is answered with the 2026 policy, or the model blends old and new rules. Effective dates make time-specific questions answerable and prevent outdated rules from leaking into current answers. It also simplifies document management: owners can upload a future policy early with an effective date, and it becomes active automatically on that date.`,
        e: `Two leave policies exist: 20 days (effective 2023-01-01 to 2026-03-31) and 24 days (from 2026-04-01). "How many days do I get?" retrieves only the current one; "How many days did employees get in 2024?" retrieves only the older one, and both answers cite the effective dates. A policy uploaded in March with effective_from 1 April started appearing in answers on 1 April automatically. Without the dates, the model had blended both policies into an answer of '20–24 days'.` },
      { t: `Compliance (GDPR, Right to be Forgotten)`,
        s: `Legal rules about personal data — lawful use, minimisation, retention limits — including deleting a person's data on request.`,
        h: `Map every place personal data can live: the database, object storage, both search indexes, caches, logs, traces, evaluation datasets, memory stores and backups. Keep lineage so you can find all of it for a person. Implement deletion and access requests across all copies within the legal deadline (often 30 days), apply retention periods, and keep an audit trail of what was done. Similar laws include India's DPDP Act and California's CCPA.`,
        u: `Apply it to any system processing personal data of covered users: HR assistants, customer-support bots, CRM-based assistants, and any system that logs user questions. Run regular deletion drills to prove the process works end to end. Keep a register of processing activities that includes the RAG system, its indexes and its logs.`,
        p: `Deleting a file in the app but leaving its chunks in a vector index, its text in logs, or its questions in an evaluation dataset violates the law and leaks data. Compliance has to be designed into ingestion and storage, not added later. It also shapes design choices early: minimising what you store, masking PII at ingestion and keeping lineage make compliance much cheaper than retrofitting it.`,
        e: `A former employee requests deletion. One job uses lineage to find their documents, chunks, vectors, cached answers, logged questions and memory entries, deletes them from every store, schedules removal from backups by retention, and records the completed request in the audit log. The request was completed in two days, well within the legal deadline, with evidence for the data-protection officer. The deletion job is the same code path used in drills, which is why it worked first time.` },
      { t: `Data Residency`,
        s: `Keeping data, and its processing, inside a required country or region.`,
        h: `Choose regions for storage, the vector database, the search engine, embedding and LLM inference, logs, traces and backups. Avoid sending text to APIs hosted elsewhere for regulated data; use self-hosted or regional models instead. Document data flows so auditors can verify that nothing crosses the boundary, including support access and monitoring tools. Encryption keys should also stay in the region, managed by the organisation rather than a foreign provider.`,
        u: `Required in government, banking, insurance, healthcare and telecom, and by data-localisation rules in the EU, India and other countries. It also matters for contracts with enterprise customers who require data to stay in their region. Include third-party tools (monitoring, tracing, support portals) in the review, since they often copy data too. Write residency requirements into procurement checklists, so new tools are checked before adoption.`,
        p: `Sending documents to a model API in another country can be illegal for regulated data, even if the API is secure. Residency requirements often decide the whole architecture: which models, which cloud regions, and whether self-hosting is necessary. It also affects vendor choice: managed services must offer the required region, and support staff access must respect it. Getting it wrong is expensive: moving an established system to another region later means migrating data, indexes and models under time pressure.`,
        e: `An Indian bank runs embeddings, the reranker and the LLM on servers in its Mumbai data centre, with backups in a second Indian region. No customer or document text leaves the country, and the architecture document shows every data flow for the regulator's audit. The same rules apply to logs and traces, which stay in the same region as the data. When a team proposed a new analytics tool hosted abroad, the checklist flagged it before any data was sent.` },
    ]},
  ],
});
